From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 11:55:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 28/28] fix(wlan): take Amazon's Echo branches for radar

The vendor driver keeps Amazon's Echo settings behind the anonymised
project names abh123/abc123/abd123: a 2.4 GHz firmware RX block-ack
window of 0x12 ("Fix Rx BA Size to 2"), power save forced to CAM, the
ARP filter off and the longer disconnect-event delay. The stock Echo
Dot kernel runs all four. Pinna builds the Echos as "radar", so none of
them ran.

Without the small window, a transfer that starts after a few idle
seconds stalls: the AP resends frames the device already received, the
firmware no longer acks them because they are behind its block-ack
scoreboard, the AP falls back to 1 Mbit/s and gives up one frame at a
time, and nothing reaches the host for 10-30 s. On an Echo Dot, 600 KB
downloads after 6 s idle each: 5 of 19 stalled with the default window,
none of 20 with 0x12, at the same 1.1-1.7 MB/s.

Match "radar" in all four checks. The abd123-only branches stay off, as
on stock. The rx_ba_win experiment switch is now unreachable and goes.
